#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} {:answer/id #uuid "3e591d12-74c2-5577-b196-484985280fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369171} {:answer/id #uuid "320ddc7c-7c7d-541e-9ebe-9ac46df72c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369172} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8f7c53-d3cc-5bfe-9343-72ec3b72a943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369173} {:answer/id #uuid "542a575b-c995-5c99-8b0f-3f774e748289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d173091-98b1-5f9e-ba14-223fe159f379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfde08b6-0b33-56dc-ab2e-672049a30b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5a5b5d-1130-562b-be75-79235b9cd42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380752} {:answer/id #uuid "d99291bd-3f8c-5335-affd-fffd94f5d90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380753} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2258da-1286-500d-b5ea-e5019f6bc9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380754} {:answer/id #uuid "80c5a22d-851a-5e64-bf18-646fa4b64de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380755} {:answer/id #uuid "2e72d255-ef28-56b9-b614-389fd1c36d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2258da-1286-500d-b5ea-e5019f6bc9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380754} :answer-field/id #uuid "a781bd1b-8b0c-5f28-b4d4-64190d3458ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a13616d8-6843-5639-b89a-041b941279af" :question/math-academy-id "q-79841" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2)=f(3.9,1.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3.9,1.2).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(3.9,1.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3.9-4=-0.1, \\\\ Δy & =1.2-1=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.1)+2\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & ≈0+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.4.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9,1.2) & ≈f(4,1)+0.4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2) & ≈\\sqrt{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e142022-0a13-5572-a090-e4c3e3f9d6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360044} {:answer/id #uuid "e093deb8-af07-57a4-a3c5-9d5cabe67cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360045} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee19c44-0e62-5f92-b07d-c2f384f63fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360046} {:answer/id #uuid "61dac524-823b-5e13-b56d-133c28aec53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac479b4-059e-536d-ab39-7951b8182714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61dac524-823b-5e13-b56d-133c28aec53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047} :answer-field/id #uuid "458fa144-24b7-5a14-a379-5e62a18ed85d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fa48839-143d-5a0b-8b2f-09125573ccac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79876" :question/problem "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c33fc4-2139-5b3c-8fc6-7729768ac716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{d}r+40\\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380759} {:answer/id #uuid "72e6ee56-8a8e-50dd-b580-d600702e778f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+10\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380760} {:answer/id #uuid "d64c152a-3ab7-5900-8f79-669eace39a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{d}r+6\\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380761} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b38237-ffab-5541-8279-f2df4c28b930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380762} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd052b9-a6fe-584b-9027-7713b15dec89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd052b9-a6fe-584b-9027-7713b15dec89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380763} :answer-field/id #uuid "015af702-09d0-5695-98c5-7791d87a48f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddfbfeb1-47d1-5e4a-929c-ad72e52c8b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79959" :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the lateral surface area $S$ of the right cone if the base radius is $r=6,$ and the slant height is $l=10.$" :question/worked-solution "The  lateral surface area of the right cone is $S=\\pi rl.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(rl) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂S}{∂l})\\,\\text{d}l \\\\  & =\\pi l\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi r\\,\\text{d}l\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6,$ and $l=10,$  the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(6,10) & =10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee984fef-dfa3-5c07-a7ad-eb2e15b08360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360051} {:answer/id #uuid "116262b2-e5ad-5d71-b1fb-0ee218f844a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360052} {:answer/id #uuid "a520640a-07a2-5431-bad2-906fbbbed2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360053} {:answer/id #uuid "413db8fc-c1b2-5ae3-8842-885ebb0c236f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360054} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8826f-291c-500b-9535-2a5b7a218e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8826f-291c-500b-9535-2a5b7a218e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360055} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff4a3a4-2864-5eb3-bd8e-28f01e3e2df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cd05d8b-6c57-5623-803a-2f4730ea02a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86715" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{3}-y),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12{z}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,-3,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,-3,1) & =(2\\cdot 2)\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y+3\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,-3+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx-\\,Δy+3\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-2-(-3)=1 \\\\ Δz & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\,Δx-1\\,Δy+3\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-2.$"}]}
